# Production overrides. Vite loads `.env` first, then this file for `npm run build`,
# and values here win — so dev keeps localhost and the production build no longer
# depends on whatever `.env` happens to be checked out on the server.
# VITE_STRIPE_PUBLIC_KEY and VITE_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID are identical in both
# environments and keep coming from `.env`.

# Deliberately EMPTY, which makes axios issue same-origin relative requests.
# Two hostnames serve this app — emailontext.com and ygbackend.com (the webhook
# host; see server/nginx/ygbackend.com for why neither redirects to the other) —
# and nginx proxies /users, /emails, /phones, /sets, /cc, /pricing on both. Any
# absolute value here would be correct for one hostname and a cross-origin call
# from the other, which the server's CORS allowlist rejects. Relative is right
# for both and takes CORS out of the browser path entirely.
VITE_API_URL=

# Must stay absolute and must match GOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI on the server byte for
# byte — it is sent on the getToken exchange and registered in the Google
# console, so it cannot vary by hostname. A Google sign-in begun on
# ygbackend.com therefore finishes on emailontext.com.
VITE_GOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI=https://emailontext.com/auth/google/callback
